<speak>
<p>Witness. Field Season One. Chapter Two: Every Kākāpō Has a Name. The kakapo.</p>
<break time="800ms"/>
<p>Here is a fact that no other animal in this Field Season can offer you: every kakapo alive has a name. Sirocco. Solstice. Hoki. Māhutonga. Not nicknames for a few favorites — all of them, each wearing a small transmitter backpack, each known personally to the rangers of New Zealand's recovery program.</p>
<p>When a species can be counted like a classroom — two hundred and thirty-six birds at the start of 2026 — you are looking at one of two things. The end of something. Or the beginning.</p>
<p>The kakapo is the world's heaviest parrot: moss-green, whiskered, smelling faintly sweet, and completely flightless. It cannot fly, but it climbs twenty-meter trees with its claws, and comes down like a parachutist with wings spread. And in 2026, this impossible bird is having the best year of its modern history.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The past. How the night parrot fell.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>For millions of years, the kakapo's enemies came from the sky, by day. So it became a creature of the night and the forest floor, and its one defense was to freeze. Perfect stillness. Perfect camouflage.</p>
<p>Then people arrived, bringing animals that hunt in the dark, by smell: rats, cats, and stoats. Every kakapo defense became a liability. The freeze that hid it from an eagle simply holds it still for a stoat. And its lovely musty scent is a beacon.</p>
<p>The fall was long and nearly total. In 1894, the conservationist Richard Henry moved hundreds of kakapo to an island refuge — and watched stoats swim across within six years and destroy them all. One of conservation's first great heartbreaks.</p>
<p>By the 1970s, decades of searching had turned up a few dozen birds. Every one of them male. The species seemed finished. Then, in 1977, about two hundred kakapo were discovered on Rakiura, Stewart Island — with females. Feral cats were already killing them, so between 1980 and 1997, every bird that could be found was evacuated to small predator-free islands.</p>
<p>In 1995, the count bottomed out at fifty-one birds. And the modern recovery program began.</p>
<p>One more name matters here. Richard Henry — not the man, the bird named in his honor. The last kakapo ever found from the Fiordland mainland, he carried genes the island flock had lost. In 1998 he fathered three chicks, before dying around 2010 at an estimated eighty years old. His line still swims in the species' blood.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The present. Where the kakapo stands.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>The count at the start of 2026 was two hundred and thirty-six, including eighty-three breeding-age females. And then the rimu trees — whose mass fruiting, every two to four years, is the only signal that makes kakapo breed — delivered a great year.</p>
<p>The first chick of the season hatched on Valentine's Day. By early April, more than ninety-five had hatched: the biggest kakapo breeding season ever recorded, beating 2019's record of seventy-three. Chicks only join the official count when they reach one hundred and fifty days, so the new total is still being earned, night by night. And some chicks have already been lost — which the program reports as plainly as the wins.</p>
<p>The recovery machine behind this is remarkable. Transmitters that flag when a female starts nesting. Feeding stations that open only to the right bird, and weigh her as she eats. And the genome of essentially every living kakapo has been sequenced, so pairings can be chosen to protect what diversity remains.</p>
<p>In 2023, ten males even returned to the New Zealand mainland for the first time in about forty years — and promptly taught everyone something by climbing the sanctuary's pest fence. Several were brought back south. And the boom of a kakapo was heard on the mainland again anyway.</p>
<p>What is known: the count is exact, because every bird is named and tracked. Breeding follows the rimu mast. The enemies are introduced predators, not mystery.</p>
<p>What is unknown: how long a kakapo lives — possibly sixty to ninety years; no one has ever watched a whole life. Why infertility and failed eggs remain stubbornly common. And the final size of this record season, still being counted. Disease is a standing risk too: in 2019, a single fungal strain reached a tenth of the species, and killed nine birds.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The future. What would improve its odds.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>The kakapo was declared all but gone three separate times, and each time, someone went looking anyway. What keeps the record year from being the last one?</p>
<p>One. More safe ground. The islands are near capacity. The great prize is Predator Free Rakiura — clearing one hundred and seventy-five thousand hectares of cats and rats would let kakapo go home, to the island where they were rediscovered. Nothing that large has ever been done.</p>
<p>Two. Keep the genetics working. Every genome is sequenced. Smart pairings and artificial insemination — once even delivered by drone — stretch the diversity of fifty-one survivors as far as it can go.</p>
<p>Three. Hold the biosecurity line. One swimming stoat undid Richard Henry's life work in 1894. Quarantine, checks, and vigilance are the unglamorous wall everything else stands behind.</p>
<p>Four. Let them be wild where possible. The program's 2026 strategy deliberately intervenes less — fewer handouts, more natural nests — aiming for birds that thrive, rather than merely survive.</p>
<p>This year, the night forests are fuller of booming than they have been in living memory. Names keep being added to the list.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>One question to carry with you. Every kakapo has a name, and it changed how people fight for them. It is harder to lose Solstice than to lose one of two hundred and thirty-six. What in your own world becomes harder to neglect, the moment you name it?</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>Every claim in this chapter is backed by a dated source, listed in the chapter text. This narration uses a synthetic voice. Thank you for witnessing.</p>
</speak>
